A||||19||Raydar||Do any 115 mm rear hubs fit Fiero?||||1||
Z||000000||Raydar||10-15-2001||01:31 PM||||Is anyone familiar with a replacement hub with a 115 mm pattern that will fit the Fiero's rear suspension and axles? I'm thinking Celebrity? 6000? <BR>Anybody?<BR>Bueller?<P>TIA. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000001||artherd||10-15-2001||02:11 PM||||If you have a pre-88, you're set, the Pontiac 6000 Whole Thing(TM) will bolt to the fiero A-arms and tie rod, etc.<P>On a 88 car, you'll have to do a fair bit of machining and fabrication (a skilled machine shop needed) as you'll be using only the larger 6000 hub.<P>I am currently working on adapting the 6000 parts to my car (it's getting a northstar, I want them for the ir extra size, and bigger bolt circle. I'll be punching them all the way to 130mm.)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||10-15-2001||04:18 PM||||Thanks, Ben. <BR>Yup. It's an 88. <BR>Do the 6000 hubs use the same three-bolt pattern, and just require a larger hole for the housing, or are they *completely* different?<BR>What I'm after is a quick and dirty way to use Grand Prix wheels. <BR>I've seen a conversion for Porsche Boxster wheels that used redrilled front hubs. Didn't look like too much of a problem. The rears looked like a royal pain, though. <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||mshill||10-15-2001||04:50 PM||||This is great info.  I am working on a rebody that needs near zero offset rims for the rearend and I think that there is a little more selection in the 115mm bolt pattern than in the 100mm pattern.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Rick 88||10-15-2001||06:19 PM||||Isn't that the pattern for those 99 and up 16in. chrome Grand Am GT fluted star wheels? Those would look nice on a Fiero!<P>Rick 88||||reg||5
Z||000005||mrfixit58||10-16-2001||07:52 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>If you have a pre-88, you're set, the Pontiac 6000 Whole Thing(TM) will bolt to the fiero A-arms and tie rod, etc.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Question: Are you referring to just the rear A-arm and tie rod or are you saying that both the front AND rear spindles/hubs are direct bolt-on replacements for the Fiero?<P>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||10-16-2001||09:23 AM||||i've been wanting to try this too, but i thought you needed some piece off a caddy too. could some one give a parts list of what to look for?<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||10-16-2001||09:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfixit58:<BR><B> Question: Are you referring to just the rear A-arm and tie rod or are you saying that both the front AND rear spindles/hubs are direct bolt-on replacements for the Fiero?<P>Roy</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Roy, I think it's just the knuckle and the hub/bearing assembly. Probably the toe links and struts are still the Fiero items. <BR>This has nothing to do with the Fiero front suspension, unless the 6000 rear hubs can also replace an 88 front hub assembly.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (Bolted through, so they won't fall apart.)<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||10-16-2001||09:46 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B> Roy, I think it's just the knuckle and the hub/bearing assembly. ... <BR>This has nothing to do with the Fiero front suspension...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I thought. What is your (or, rather, everyone else's) idea on converting the front to 115 mm?<P>Roy<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||10-16-2001||10:34 AM||||Roy,<BR>This is where I got my idea. Someone else posted this, a while back.<BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A> <P>This person put Porsche Boxster (5x130    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> ) wheels on an 88. <BR>I would be happy with 115's. <BR>The front hubs don't look like too much of a problem. I don't want to go through all the  crap for the rears, that he did, though. <BR>Hence, my question regarding the 6000 pieces. <P>If you don't have an 88, you might be able to have the earlier front hub/rotor assembly redrilled for 115. Might even be easier than all this other madness. <BR>All just speculation.<BR>If you do this, and your rotor breaks in half, I <I>know nothing</I>.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||10-16-2001||02:07 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Roy,<BR>This is where I got my idea. Someone else posted this, a while back.<BR> <A HREF="http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fiero.cc/fiero-tdc/members/mws/Boxster/index.html</A> <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Got it. Thanks. It looks like a simple procedure to have the front hubs re-drilled.<P>Roy||||reg||14
Z||000011||Bob||10-16-2001||04:14 PM||||So what parts are needed to convert a 85-87 Fiero to 115mm bolt pattern? I would love to be able to use my extra set of Gp rims.<P>------------------<BR>1997 Pontiac Grand Prix GTP -lotsa mods, so little room...||||reg||5
Z||000012||lowCG||10-17-2001||09:28 PM||||After what I've just gone through with some front hubs,I wouldn't want to re-drill anything into that weak casting.<BR>With the setup involved in re-drilling,wouldn't it be better to make some adaptors?||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||10-18-2001||08:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>After what I've just gone through with some front hubs,I wouldn't want to re-drill anything into that weak casting.<BR>With the setup involved in re-drilling,wouldn't it be better to make some adaptors?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Can you expound on this? Specifically, what kind of problems have you just gone trough?<P>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||lowCG||10-18-2001||08:49 PM||||I had to find another set of hub/rotors to use in my front brake conversion after the replacement studs cracked the cast material around the studs.This was due to only .005" of excess(beyond what was proper) interference between the stud and hub.The studs were turned down,and welded from behind on my current set.<BR>The hub material is very brittle and weak,to increase the bolt circle would require the stud bores to be in a part of the casting never meant to accept such loads,and I guess I'd be scared after working with these parts.<BR>A spacer/adapter would allow more clearance for improved brakes also.<BR>Try taking an old rotor and drilling into it close to the edge,then put a bolt through the hole and hit it with a hammer and you'll see what I mean.<BR>If there was just one part on the car that deserves a billet steel replacement,it'd have to be the front hubs.||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||10-25-2001||08:17 AM||||I was in the bone-yard last week and was studying the hubs on the 6000 and a couple of questions came to mind...<P>- Will the hubs accept the Fiero rear calipers if I re-drilled the rotors for a 115 mm bolt pattern?<P>- If I decide to us the vented rotors from the 6000, is there a caliper that can be adapted so I can still have a parking brake?<P>- I remember reading an article about using the larger rear Cadillac rotors and calipers (with the emergence brake option). Would this conversion work on the 6000 hub as well as the Fiero?<P>Thanks,<P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||5
Z||000016||Raydar||10-25-2001||09:33 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>I had to find another set of hub/rotors to use in my front brake conversion after the replacement studs cracked the cast material around the studs.This was due to only .005" of excess(beyond what was proper) interference between the stud and hub.The studs were turned down,and welded from behind on my current set.<BR>The hub material is very brittle and weak,to increase the bolt circle would require the stud bores to be in a part of the casting never meant to accept such loads,and I guess I'd be scared after working with these parts.<BR>A spacer/adapter would allow more clearance for improved brakes also.<BR>Try taking an old rotor and drilling into it close to the edge,then put a bolt through the hole and hit it with a hammer and you'll see what I mean.<BR>If there was just one part on the car that deserves a billet steel replacement,it'd have to be the front hubs.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LowCG, <BR>I know this doesn't address your situation directly, but this may not be a problem on the 88. I think the front hub is machined steel instead of cast iron.<BR>The guy in the link posted previously didn't seem to have any trouble with redrilled 88 front hubs. Said he tried to break the hub by levering against the stud. Broke the stud instead. <BR>I'm now wondering if the Fiero rear hubs can be redrilled for 115. May be too small.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 10-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||artherd||10-26-2001||12:54 AM||||Yeah, the front hubs on 88 cars are rather stronger pieces aparently...<P>The outer edge of the stud hole on the Boxster Wheels Man's car was only 3mm from the lip... <P>Bent, then broke the stud, not the hub.<P>Bearings themselves on the fronts are a little small for my taste for my brake upgrade project (rather extreme  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> though.<P>I am working on using spindles from the 6000, redrilled out to 5x130mm, at all 4 corners of the car.  We will see!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>  LowCG, <BR>I know this doesn't address your situation directly, but this may not be a problem on the 88. I think the front hub is machined steel instead of cast iron.<BR>The guy in the link posted previously didn't seem to have any trouble with redrilled 88 front hubs. Said he tried to break the hub by levering against the stud. Broke the stud instead. <BR>I'm now wondering if the Fiero rear hubs can be redrilled for 115. May be too small.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000018||lowCG||10-26-2001||07:20 PM||||Wow,if I'd known that about the '88 hubs,I would've tried to adapt them to my brake project,since the caliper brackets weren't made yet.<BR>Have been driving around with the new big brakes,and they're great fun.The rear is still stock,and will get an upgrade eventually,but for now,at least,the car is safer to drive than with the old brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Will||11-23-2001||12:22 AM||||I'm doing the 115 swap this weekend on my '87 GT.<P>For now I will use P6K hub carriers, hubs, rotors, and calipers. I will change over to Caddilac parts when I find out what fits.<P>The cast aluminum rear hub carriers from the new Malibus might be workable for a 115mm rear swap into an '88 Fiero. The suspension is similar: struts with two lateral links and a trailing arm, although the trailing arm is attached differently. The Malibu trailing arm would have to be extended forward to the Fiero pivot, or a totally new custom arm made. The camber bolt holes would have to be drilled because the Malibu uses smaller camber bolts than the Fiero. I don't know if the camber bolt holes in the Malibu's hub carriers are aligned correctly to give the appropriate range of camber adjustment on a Fiero, though. There is probably a drive axle bearing which can be substituted for the Malibu's non-drive bearing.<BR>Up to '99 Malibus had rear drums. From 2000 on, they have disks.<P>||||reg||1
